le is an example your pledge you  said end Outsourcing on our NHS you've  changed your mind yeah well look the  Outsourcing of some issues and functions  I don't think has been very effective  but if you take the NHS then it's just  as always used GPS in private practice  that's always been part of it for many  many years the NHS has referred NHS  um you know patience to the private  sector to have operations hip operations  knee operations Etc I think we could be  more effective at that but I'm not  talking about privatizing the NHS the  same is true in criminal justice I do  think it should be run as a public  service but it doesn't mean there aren't  um there isn't a private sector in there  self-employed barristers for example  from the private sector provide a  service in criminal justice in relation  to prosecutions what I want to ensure  that is that our Public Service which  frankly are on their knees I mean you  know ask your I I mean you know ask  yourself the question is any public  service now better than it was 13 years  ago when this government came in and I  don't think anybody with a straight face  could answer that question and say yes  they're better than they were no nor  could anyone frankly answer the question  do you feel personally better off over  the last 13 years or is our economy  better off almost all those questions  Peg only one answer which is no you um I  was quite interested earlier in the  interview when we talk about the NHS and  you were saying about how your wife  tells you stories about what's going on  what kind of thing did she say well you  know  probably what everybody working in the  NHS so I mean my sister used to work in  the NHS my mum worked at NHS so I know a  lot about it and obviously a lot of  people we know work in the NHS but it's  completely under strain one of the big  big issues is Workforce and that's why  it's very important that we made that  commitment end on Dom status use that  money to train up the Next 