ar is that too low or too high  well look so if you're not going to get  into a numbers game we need Fair rules  um firmly applied and we need to  distinguish between the different  reasons that people come to this country  some people come to join their families  I think broadly speaking most people  accept that that's perfectly all right a  number comes to students now all the  best students from across the world to  come here some arrive as refugees  including from places like like  Afghanistan and Ukraine and obviously we  welcome them as we always have done and  some come to work now what I've said in  a speech I did earlier in fact at the  tail end of last year is that when it  comes to those coming to work of course  we recognize and welcome the  contribution that everybody who comes to  this country makes but what I've said to  the CBI is we need to wean ourselves off  this sense that rather than skill up and  train people in this country we can  simply bring people in from other  countries I don't think that's right I  think that we can do far better on the  skills in this country so I think  there's room for fair rules for firmly  applied and for a national debate about  what we want from immigration one of the  reasons I'm Keen to get you a view on  this is because it feels to be that you  have shifted I guess on immigration  again in in the last few years so in the  leadership campaign we can see on one  side of the screen one of your pledges  was to defend free movement as we leave  the EU of elidship pledge in 2020 and  then compare that to a melon Sunday  interview freedom of movement is a red  line for me it won't come back well  let's just look at those these are  diametrically opposed of course they're  not look defend free movement as we  leave the EU right we've left no so now  we've got to face the future we're in  we're out of the EU free movement is  gone so the question then comes well  what do you want now do you want to go  back or do you want to have a different  system with Fair rules except this is as  we leave the EU but this is this is  acknowledging that we're leaving the EU  and saying that you'll be defending free  movement as we leave but we've left  we've left and therefore free moment  free movement was part of the package  for being a member of the EU inevitably  um we've left free movement has come to  an end now for a different question  arises which is what immigration system  do you want now and I think we should  have a fair system with Fair rules that  are firmly applied and that's what we  would devise so it's nothing to do with  the fact that in the leadership campaign  you were pitching to pro-remain labor  members and now you're again looking at  those swing voters many of them who  voted brexit if you don't like my  principles I have others there's nothing  to do with that I think so here I mean  it's obvious what that pledge was as we  leave the EU we've left the EU we've got  to face the future as it is but I see  this the other thing that I said when we  did lots and lots of speeches and  hostings uh materials in that leadership  campaign my overwhelming pitch the labor  party was we have to win the next  election otherwise none of the things  we're talking about are kind of come to  pass and we've lost elections  consistently since 20 10 I'm determined  that that won't happen again and that's  why I set myself when I became leader of  this party just under three years ago  three tasks the first was to change the  labor party when you lose as badly as we  did in 2019 you don't look at the  electorate and say what were you doing  you look at your party and say we're  going to change and we've been really  clear about the change that we've  brought about within the party well  that's anti-Semitism whether that's NATO  whether that's our relationship with  business the second thing is we have to  expose the government is not fit to  govern well you can see what 13 years of  failure has done the next stage is to  lay out the alternative we began to do  that last year and 2023 is this chance  for us to set out would a bold reforming  labor government will do let's just have  a look at a picture of you this week  giving your speech if I'm a you looked  pretty relaxed and confident when you  gave the speech you know you had no  jacket you had your sleeves rolled up  you know is that the look of a man who  thinks he's going to be the next prime  minister I hope so yes um what I was  doing there was setting out  the case for 2023 for Hope for Change  and the sort of bold change double we  need a decade of national renewal and I  think  um the next election is going to be  about change and you know if anybody  asks themselves am I better off than I  was in 2010 when this government first  came in I think the answer will be no  are our Public Services any better now  than they were then the answer will be  no is our economy working better now  than it was then the answer will be no  so we desperately need change what I  want to do is to set out that case for  change not not just what the change will  be but also how we're going to bring it  now looking ahead to 2023 you can have  time to read the Prince Harry book I  don't know I haven't uh set aside time  yet are you sympathetic with him or well  my governing principle is that  politicians shouldn't Wade in to private  matters of the royal family I know some  private at the minute right well but  some politicians like the way he didn't  give opinions left right and Center I  actually don't and I think it's better  not to Wade in maybe this one then it's  2023 the year that Arsenal going to win  the Premier League I really hope so  we're we're so tight at the top  um we've got to keep going it's a  fantastic Squad really good team of  players there we're in a very very good  position
 

